In article <1993May31.021234.65883@qut.edu.au>, eep300dorair@qut.edu.au writes:
>     Can someone direct me where I can obtain information
>     on DECNET protocols (LAT,ROUTING,MOP,LAVC etc).

I answered this by mail, but here is the info for anyone else who's interested.
(Is there a big DECnet implementation effort in Australia?  This is the second
request of this kind within a week!)

The DECnet Phase IV protocol documentation is orderable from DEC via the 
following part numbers:

Phase IV General Description AA-N149A-TC
4.0 DDCMP AA-D588A-TC
4.1 DDCMP AA-K176A-TK
2.0 Ethernet (the blue book) AA-K759B-TK
1.0 Ethernet DL Func Spec AA-Y298A-TK
1.0 Ethernet Product Arch Spec AA-X440A-TK
2.0 Routing Layer Func Spec AA-X435A-TK
3.2 NSP Func Spec AA-K176A-TK
4.0 NSP Func Spec AA-X439A-TK
1.0 Session Control Func Spec AA-K182A-TK
5.6 DAP Func Spec AA-K177A-TK
2.0 NVT Foundation Services AA-DY89A-TK
2.0 NVT CTERM AA-DY88A-TK
4.0 Net Mgmt Func Spec AA-X437A-TK
3.0 MOP Func Spec AA-X436A-TK

(or at least these used to be orderable; I haven't checked lately.)

LAT and LAVC are (a) not part of DECnet and (b) considered proprietary by DEC;
you can't simply order up the documentation on them.  They will sell the info 
on LAT for a hefty license fee.  I don't know that LAVC is available under ANY
terms, though some mfrs of Ethernet protocol analyzers seem to know something
about it.  Just for grins, though, you might try ordering AA-NL26A-TE, 
"Local Area Transport (LAT) Specification", and see what happens...

