         DECUS '93/ATLANTA TO FEATURE EXTENSIVE MICROSOFT CONTENT

          Reflects DECUS U.S. Chapter Commitment To Heterogeneous
                       Computing Session Offerings  


SHREWSBURY, Mass., May 26, 1993 --  Illustrating its commitment to provide 
information for multiple platform integration, the U.S. Chapter of the 
Digital Equipment Computer Users Society (DECUS) will feature extensive 
Microsoft related topics at its session offerings at DECUS '93/Atlanta in 
June.

This content reflects the growing importance of multiplatform environments 
and the industry's need for objective education about heterogeneous 
computing topics.  Microsoft and Digital have forged a seven-year 
relationship that provides the industry with powerful corporate computing 
solutions.

"DECUS represents a strategic opportunity for Microsoft because this users 
society embodies Digital's customer base ... yesterday, today and tomorrow.  
In particular, DECUS '93/Atlanta gives Microsoft an efficient way to extend 
beyond the desktop user to reach an audience focused on the enterprise-wide 
corporate computing environment.  As DECUS continues to transform and 
embrace a heterogeneous computing environment, the relevancy of Microsoft's 
product line to DECUS members will broaden even more," said Chris Phinney, 
Microsoft national account manager for Digital.

Reflecting this cooperative effort, DECUS '93/Atlanta will feature over 75 
hours of in-depth technical content sessions that have direct Microsoft 
relevancy.  These sessions are not only conducted by Microsoft and Digital, 
but also by several other companies.    

Representative Microsoft content includes:
* Windows NT Operating System & Related Products
* Windows Operating System
* Windows For Workgroups  
* Microsoft Mail
* LAN Manager
* MS-DOS 6 Operating System
* Visual Basic Programming System
* Visual C++ Development System
* C++
* FoxPro Database
* Microsoft Access DBMS
* Enterprise connecting in a multivendor environment
* SNA
* SQL Server
* Client Server

Practical Insight For Multiplatform Integration

"Although there are many trade shows and technical conferences, few 
opportunities match DECUS '93/Atlanta for efficiency and Return On 
Investment (ROI).  In one week, attendees can acquire a level of 
understanding that enables them to immediately get more return out of their 
computing investment," said Margaret Knox, president of DECUS, U.S. 
Chapter.

"DECUS delivers this ROI because it is focused on real-world knowledge, not 
theory and marketing hype.  Our Microsoft content illustrates this 
commitment.  DECUS '93/Atlanta is a place where people will get the 
practical insight they need to integrate across multiple platforms," said 
Knox.

Attendees will have the opportunity to interact face-to-face with 
representatives from Digital and Digital-related organizations as well as 
fellow imdustry professionals. By creating this environment, DECUS 
'93/Atlanta will help users significantly enhance the sizable investment 
made in their existing products, Knox explained.

In addition to the extensive Microsoft content, participants will also be 
able to acquire expertise in other computing areas.  These include 
enterprise engineering, user-centered design, computer rightsizing, 
designing graphical user interfaces and databases in an open, global 
networked environment.

DECUS '93/Atlanta will be held June 5-11 at the Georgia World Congress 
Center in Atlanta. The Microsoft related sessions will be held along with 
over 1,100 technical symposium sessions, over 100 technical half-, one- and 
two-day seminars, the Digital Technology Center and the premiere of a new 
multivendor trade show.

On-site registration will be conducted throughout the week in Hall E of the 
Georgia  World Congress Center. More information can be acquired by calling 
1-800-DECUS55.

DECUS members solve their organizations' computing problems and stay 
informed about industry issues and emerging technologies with the aid of 
the U.S. Chapter's semiannual national seminars, symposium and trade show 
event; regional conferences; local seminars program; Special Interest and 
Local Users Groups; electronic conferencing system; and public domain 
library. 

