>Hi Netters,

>One of my friends doing Ph.d in India needs a powerful vector processing
>machine for his project work. The research institue where he is studying has
>narrowed down the choices to DEC-3000-400AXP and HP-735. My friend wants to
>know which one is better (reliability, performance etc). If you have any
>experience with the machines referred, please give your advice. If you have
>any benchmark results, please let me know.

      I do not think either of those are vector processors.  They run
very well in-cache, but I do not think out-of-cache performance is as
good.  Vector processors have the advantage that they typically run
faster as your data gets bigger (usually you have to avoid special
numbers like powers of 64, but that usually isn't very difficult). 
Various techniques can help you make your code run in-cache, but it
isn't always practical.  The POWER (aka RS/6000) can maintain very good
performance for single-stride data access, but it isn't exactly a Cray
Y-MP C90...  The price on all of these is less than a Cray Y-MP EL. 
Anybody have numbers?  
-- 
Benjamin Z. Goldsteen


