It's been awhile, but just for clarification,...  There was some mention of
'cards being able to see each other and talk to each other...' (not by don).
On LSI (Qbus machines {being discussed}), grant continuity is only required
for DMA cards and Boot Devices (same thing/reason).  So, you can take a non-dma
card and place it in slot 9/AB or 9/CD and use it just fine.  Further, even
dma cards which can be used in non dma mode, and have a non-dma software
driver for it, the card will work anywhere.  There are a _very__few_ cards
which to not use dma, and can NOT be between DMA cards in a functioning
system.  By the same token, grant continuity must be maintained across the
entire chain of the backplane grant continuity path.  There _are_ some back-
planes which have strange paths (z or x or things), and the path must be
known or figured out if lots of cards are needed or odd placements desired.
Given the example that don shows above, with a typical, top-down jump top-down
path, a card which is to be placed in 3/CD or 6/CD which needs grant continuity
would have to have buss grant jumpers in 7/AB, 8/AB, 9/AB, and 3/CD (for 6/CD).
There is a single reason I can think of to do such a strange thing, and that
is to compensate for a bad/weak/faulty power supply in a BA2* box.

Also, if the subject is twisted a tad, and Unibus Machines (aka 11/04,11/34,
11/44,11/70) are being discussed, grant continuity jumpers (flip chips) are
needed everywhere.  The chain is a zig-zag I think, and empty AB CD EF slots
all need jumpers.  (I also think this might only be true only for older
{oldest} systems which haven't received all the FCO rev's).





