This question keeps getting asked, in various forms, in every comp.sys.*
newsgroup, several times each week.  Since I'm tired of seeing the same
back-and-forth specsmanship (specspersonship) in the replies, I'll throw
in my own $0.03 (inflation, you know) worth:

There's only one good answer to your question: Run your own benchmarks using
your code!

I've been through this many many times, and the answer always is that the
performance of a computer on your code is usually within a factor of ten
of what would be predicted from SPEC ratings etc., but is often more than
a factor of two different.

For example, we've been benchmarking an Alpha AXP 3000/500 against an IBM
RS-6000/320.  We've gotten speed ratios from 2:1 to 11:1 depending on the
particular program being run.

