In article <1993May21.203141.1533@Rapnet.Sanders.Lockheed.Com>, cupak@rapnet.sanders.lockheed.com (John J Cupak Jr CCP) writes:
> 
> I am developing a Pascal system under VAX/VMS, and am having problems
> trying to compile across the network.
> 
> The workstation I use is connected to a cluster with all of the latest
> DECset tools (LSE, SCA, MMS, PCA, and DTM), but without a Pascal
> compiler.
> 
> I would like to use LSE to edit the Pascal source file on the
> workstation, then use the LSE COMPILE command to submit the Pascal 
> source file for compilation on a *different node* which has the 
> Pascal compiler. 
> 
> This is *somewhat* like submitting a batch job to perform a compilation:
... <stuff deleted>
> 
> If Anyone has figured out how to submit a compilation on one machine to
> run on another machine, please e-mail me your solution.

A scheme I devised a few years ago to meet this need goes like this:

In LOGIN.COM or SYLOGIN.COM:
    $ FOR*TRAN :== @somewhere:COMPILE FORTRAN
    $ CC       :== @somewhere:COMPILE CC

then somewhere:COMPILE.COM is
$! Compile C or FORTRAN on PEBBLE
$! Usage:
$!    $ @compile CC/FORTRAN parameters and switches
$ place = F$ENVIRONMENT("DEFAULT")
$ file = p2 + ".*"
$ IF F$SEARCH(file) .NES. ""
$ THEN
$    comline = """''p1' ''p2' ''p3' ''p4' ''p5' ''p6'"""
$    SUBMIT/QUEUE=pebble_batch/NOPRINT/KEEP/LOG='place''p1'.LOG -
       /PAR=('place','comline')/NOTIFY somewhere:PEBBLE_COMPILE.COM
$    WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Compiler output in ''p1'.LOG"
$ ELSE
$    WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "File not found: ''p2'.*"
$ ENDIF

and somewhere:PEBBLE_COMPILE.COM
$! For remote submission of compilation from other nodes
$! See COMPILE.COM
$ SET VERIFY
$ SET DEF 'p1'
$ 'p2'

This, of course, assumes that your workstation is in the same cluster as the
node where the compiler resides.  If not, you would need to add code to
copy the source files, etc.  Also, use of network task or 0 object might
be somewhat more elegant than submitting a batch job, and is probably the
way that I would do it now.

