You're confusing things by using "A", "B" and "C" to refer to "slots".
Q-Bus gurus generally refer to slots by number, and use "A-B" to refer
to the "left-hand" half and "C-D" to the right-hand half of each slot
(for quad-width slots, with slot 1 at the top).

For half-width backplanes (the LSI-11/2) and all full-width backplanes,
the A-B slots are a true bus - all lines wired in parallel to all slots
(except for interrupt lines).  For most full-width backplanes, some of
the slots use the "C-D bus" interconnect for the right half.  The C-D
bus is a board-to-board interconnect between adjacent slots.

This gives a wiring diagram like the following (with slot 1 on the left,
and the A-B bus on the bottom):

D   |___| |___| |___| |___ - - -

C   |___| |___| |___| |___ - - -

B  |_____|_____|_____|____ - - -

A  |_____|_____|_____|____ - - -

   1     2     3     4     - - -

For those backplanes where the CD bus only connects the first three slots,
the regular Q-bus runs down the AB side up to slot 4, then runs in a
serpentine fashion: 4CD-5CD-5AB-6AB-6CD-7CD-7AB-....

