In article <1tvh5iINNs6k@flash.pax.tpa.com.au>, msmith@flash.pax.tpa.com.au (miff) writes:
> I understand that the RD53 is a somewhat peculiar unit - does anyone have
> any suggestions as to what should be done to coerce this drive to work?
> 
> I will probably be trying on an RQDX3 in a few days (I expect it will be
> an F3 board with the 339/340 ROM set) - any comments on this?

  The RQDX1/2 boards used hard-coded (in controller ROM) tests to figure
out what drive type was attached. They do this by issuing various operations
which may not be legal on the drive (select head 7, seek cylinder 1023, etc.).
Since the RD53 has 1024 cylinders, 8 heads none of these work - a "drive"
that simply said "Ok" to all of these tests would be configured as an RD53.

  So, DEC does something different to tell if it's an RD53. In order for a
"real" RD53 to ID itself to the RQDX1/2, it needs to have a jumper inserted
at R7 on the the drive logic board.

  The RQDX3 abandoned all of those games, and if the formatter can't guess
what drive type the drive is, it prompts the user to enter the correct value.

