Try it with +O3 +ESlit -Wl,-a,archive; I've found those options produce the
fastest code, at least for what I've worked with.  The +O3 gives the highest
optimization, the +ESlit puts string literals in read-only memory (better for
write-back cache when they are read) and the -Wl,-a,archive uses archive
libraries instead of shared libraries, which reduces start-up overhead as
well as the overhead of calling them.  I'd be interested to see how a 735
does with these options, that would be about as good as an HP 735 can get.

(Nevermind that this whole thread is pretty silly, we are trading a single
benchmark of dubious quality for the whole SPEC suite which is presumably
better :-) )

