dow@kpc.com (Chris Dow) writes:
>	All this about OSF/1 on Alpha's vexes me.  Unless you are writing 
>drivers, why do you care what's in the kernel?

	I care a lot. There are qualitative differences between some
kernel implementations. For example, some versions of UNIX running on
approximately similar hardware show wildly varying I/O throughput.
This is worth being concerned about. Some versions of UNIX running
on approximately similar hardware have interesting technological
features that others lack - for example paged-backed memory file
systems, journaling file systems, or disk striping and volume
shadowing. Some versions of UNIX have egregiously stupid kernel
bugs left over from BSD4.2 that the vendor hasn't fixed yet because
they rely on customers who don't care what's in the kernel unless
they are writing drivers.

	This isn't just an OSF-versus-anything issue: it behooves
you to care about your kernel and the quality of its implementation
and its features.

mjr.


