In article <jockcC7C2ns.Eqz@netcom.com> jockc@netcom.com (jockc) writes:
>Question 2:  I have a CDROM with OSF-1 on it, and I'm wondering about
>how long/short easy/pain-in-the-ass the installation procedure is? 

I've installed OSF from the CD-ROM several times.  Starting from
power-on to a fully-installed system takes under 2 hours of elapsed
time, including all the local customization that I do.  For most of
the 2 hours, you don't have do actually do anything (only a few dozen
questions need to be answered).

I've heard that upgrading to a new release now takes less than 20
minutes, but I haven't tried that myself.

>Also, can I have Open VMS and OSF-1 both installed?  This machine has
>3 disks I believe.

If you are careful to keep the disks separate, there should be no
trouble.  Of course, if you configure OpenVMS to write on an OSF disk,
or vice versa, you'd better have recent backup tapes.  To switch from
one system to the other, you would have to change the console's default
boot parameters (a single command will do this).

Caveat: I've never tried to do this, but I don't see why it wouldn't work.

You could always set up two disks as unit 0, and then unplug the VMS
disk when running OSF (or vice versa).

-Jeff


