I am developing a Pascal system under VAX/VMS, and am having problems
trying to compile across the network.

The workstation I use is connected to a cluster with all of the latest
DECset tools (LSE, SCA, MMS, PCA, and DTM), but without a Pascal
compiler.

I would like to use LSE to edit the Pascal source file on the
workstation, then use the LSE COMPILE command to submit the Pascal 
source file for compilation on a *different node* which has the 
Pascal compiler. 

This is *somewhat* like submitting a batch job to perform a compilation:

1. The REMOTE_COMPILE.COM command file:

	$! REMOTE_COMPILE.COM
	$!
	$! CREATE BATCHFILE WITH FILENAME AND OPTIONS
	$!
	$  OPEN /WRITE BATCHFILE BATCHFILE.COM
	$  WRITE       BATCHFILE "$ PASCAL ",P1," ",P2
	$  CLOSE       BATCHFILE
	$!
	$! SUBMIT JOB TO SYS$BATCH
	$! 
	$  SUBMIT/NOPRINT/DELETE BATCHFILE.COM
	$!
	$! WAIT UNTIL BATCH JOB COMPLETES
	$!
	$  SYNCHRONIZE BATCHFILE

2. In the buffer Test.pas, use the COMPILE editor command:

	LSE> COMPILE @REMOTE_COMPILE

3. The COMPILE command caused the Editor to write Test.pas to the disk,
   then spawn a subprocess the execute the following DCL command:

	@REMOTE_COMPILE Test.pas /DIAGNOSTICS

4. The procedure will create the BATCHFILE.COM command file containing 
   the following DCL command:

	$ PASCAL TEST.PAS /DIAGNOSTICS

   The procedure will also submit BATCHFILE.COM to run in batch.

If Anyone has figured out how to submit a compilation on one machine to
run on another machine, please e-mail me your solution.

Thanks,
John

