In article <9305170646.AA08666@mellorn.ipmce.su>, zaitcev@ipmce.su (Pete A. Zaitcev) writes:
> Whould anybody tell me, is there any RAM on the KA630 CPU (MV-II)?
> I am interested also to know can it be plugged into Q-bus chasis
> (I hope to use it in order to upgrade my LSI-11).

  There were two kinds of KA630 module - one with 256Kb of memory (this
version is quite rare) and one with 1Mb of memory.

  Add-on memory boards come in 1, 2, 4, 8, and 16Mb versions (not all
sizes are available from all vendors), and the system supports up to 2
add-on memory boards and a total of 16Mb of memory. Normally, this was
achieved with 2 8Mb memory boards (the CPU disables the on-board memory
if 16Mb is present in the system).

  The KA630 requires a C-D backplane cabinet, as found in the first few
slots of the BA23 and BA123 cabinets, all slots (I think) of the BA2xx
cabinets, and in one version of a BA11 cabinet. If you plug the CPU into
a non-C-D slot or into a system where something else is on the C-D bus,
you will almost certainly destroy the CPU board (and quite possibly any-
thing else which is on the C-D bus).

