In article <1tbkrm$h14@agate.berkeley.edu>, avk@hafnium.cchem.berkeley.edu (Tony Konashenok) writes:
> A while ago, someone said that RQDX2 (as opposed to RQDX3)
> supports RD51-53 and RX50 only. Is there any firmware version
> in RQDX2 supporting RD54, RD31, RX33, RX23? (Underground
> upgrades will also do the job).

  The RQDX1 and RQDX2 are essentially the same board, although it was cleaned
up for the 2. Of course, the firmware is different as well, although the 2's
firmware will happily run in an RQDX1.

  The RQDX1/2 board doesn't have the ability to generate cylinder addresses
> 1023, which you'd need for the RD54/XT-2190, which has 1224 (+ 2 CE) cyl-
inders. It also can't generate the higher floppy data rate needed for the 
RX33, etc. I'm not sure if it passes side select for floppies, either.

  This means that of your list, it could possibly be hacked to do the RD3x
drives, but that's about it.

  With the B2-RQDX3-M board available from DEC for $350 (refurbed), or from
various used equipment dealers for about $150, it's hardly worth the effort.
And if you really need faster/bigger storage, it would make more sense to get
out of the ST-506 interface and go SCSI or ESDI. Personally, I think a board
for the Q-bus that had MSCP emulation and connected to 2 IDE drives would be
a big seller in the low-end market - the savings on the drive would make up
for the cost of the board. We could even add a 4Mb SIMM ($140 at your local
computer store) and some other goodies and make a complete multifunction
board for hobbyist PDP-11's that would only need a CPU to be a full system.
Anybody want to start a design team? 8-)))

