>Indigo R3000 (!):  12.5 seconds
>Alpha:             10.9 seconds
>HP735:              5.9 seconds

	SGI is real sharp about keeping up with the latest and best
compiler/optimizer tricks. They've also just generally done a pretty
good job with optimizing various oft-called library routines(*).

	Back when I was at DEC I did a benchmark between an Indigo
and a DECSystem5000/240. Based on the clock rates of the systems,
one would expect the DECSystem to have been a mite under twice as
fast as the Indigo. The Indigo came in 6 times faster. Turns out
the version of malloc() that ships with ULTRIX is real slow. Once
malloc() was replaced, the SGI and the DEC machines ran about par,
with the DEC machine a hair faster.

	Since both the DEC machine and the SGI had the MIPS compiler
suite, I ran the application under pixie and looked at the profiling
results. The DECSystem executed 1.7 times as many instructions as
the SGI to do the same work. Even stuff like fprintf(), the SGI spent
less instructions in. Often dramatically less.

	The compiler guys DEC has working on Alpha compilers are
some really hot hackers. Remember that the Alpha compilers are a lot
newer than the MIPS compiler suite - once they get ironed out, look
for some major performance improvements.

	Moral:
		Work smarter not harder.
		Track compiler technologies.
		Profile and optimize libc and stdio.

mjr.

(* I have not seen source. I *assume* they have done this based on
how fast even basic stdio stuff performs)


