In article <1993May19.074447.1@ug.eds.com>, riceg@ug.eds.com writes:
> Are you SURE? a 750 requires a 30 amp service, and generates LOTS of
> Btus (that need a LOT of air conditioning). A 780 needs 220v 3 phase
> service and even MORE air conditioning.

  No, the 750 has a 120V 30A plug and the 78x has a 208V 3 phase plug.
Both only use 120V single phase internally, and they consume a good
deal less current than you'd believe from the plugs. I posted some info
about this to the net some time ago. While I don't have the exact cur-
rent requirements for the 78x handy, I did measure a VAX 8650, which
DEC provides with a 60A 3 phase plug which can be used as a deadly
weapon 8-). I got 12.3A, 13.1A, 14.8A on the 3 phases. I made an adapter
using the 60A receptacle and a L5-21P (I think that's it) 30A plug, like
the one on a 78x. I then ran the 8650 off the same circuit that was run-
ning an HSC50 for testing. After we got the 8650 all set up, we moved it
to the other side of the machine room and replaced a 785. We're still
using my adapter, plugged into the receptacle that served the 785, with
a 30A breaker. Works fine.

  The 750 will happily work on a normal 20A receptacle. The 78x will
be quite happy on 2 20A circuits (watch the neutral current, though!).

  Anyone seriously interested in running these systems on non-standard
wiring should invest in a clip-on current probe. I got a nice one from
Newark Electronics for under $100. This will let you see what the de-
vice *really* draws "in it's native habitat", and you can then decide
how to proceed.

> Do you have a facility capable of providing these things?

  I don't know about him - I've seen the same posting for an extended
time now, yet he hasn't replied to me about 2 785's I have. Oh well.

