|>
|>Has anybody successfully run SCSI disks or RAID array off the Vax4100
|>SCSI port ??
|>
|>DEC's options catalog doesn't say it can be done, but doesn't say it can
|>either.
|>
|>They do say however, that you can connect SCSI Tape backup devices from
|>the 4100 SCSI port.
|>Surely disks would be ok too ?!
|>
|>Thanks,
|>Jim.
|>


The SCSI port on the VAX 4000-100 is a normal SCSI port (not fast and not
wide).  I would expect that most commercial SCSI disks would work, although
boot support is an entirely different issue.  Many SCSI disks don't follow the
SCSI protocols exactly, and since the boot protocol is not specifically
designed to gracefully handle all the quirks of all SCSI drives in the world,
there is a chance that you won't be able to boot off third party drives.  
I believe that the VMS driver is a little more "forgiving" when it comes to 
handling third party drives.

 
I have personally seen this with Fujitsu drives.  The drives seem to work 
under VMS, but the boot protocol fails because the drive does something 
unexpected.

I personally recommend using the supported DEC drives for this reason, as I 
have seen other customers run into problems with third party drives after 
shelling out their money for them.

Regards,

>>>Joe


