Interesting.  I remember back a few years when experts were writing off
the use of Silicon for processors because of the limitations of physics
stopped faster processors from being produced.  All effort was being
put into Galliun Arsenide as the new processor base.  Shortly later, Apollo
figured out their floating point unit, and suddenly IBM, HP, DEC, Intel,
and others have chips that bypass the old concerns (poor Apollo couldn't
stop the others from pirating their ideas... :-)

The moral of the story:  Buy for today, buy UNIX, and buy from a company
that is *currently* offering reasonable board-swap upgrades...  Be ready
to swap vendor loyalty yearly.  The "best chip" lead lasts 1/2 year. 

