In article <1tejns$2uf@sol.TIS.COM> mjr@tis.com (Marcus J Ranum) writes:
>>Indigo R3000 (!):  12.5 seconds
>>Alpha:             10.9 seconds
>>HP735:              5.9 seconds

Various benchmarks we have run here give the indication that our Crimson
(roughly the equivalent of an R4000 Indigo) is very roughly on par with
our HP720 (some things are better on the Crimson, some are better on the
HP).  The SGI has the advantage on larger jobs due to three reasons: disk
I/O is faster, the 1Meg secondary cache, and it has three times the
memory (96Meg vs 32Meg on the HP).  We expect the HP735s we have on order
to have much I/O that will be faster than our SGI (to be fair to SGI this
is because we have old slow recycled drives on it), and with twice the
clock speed and other improvements in the CPU (plus having 64Meg of RAM)
will result in the benchmarks running much faster on the HP than the SGI.

X-Windows performance is real good on the HP, but if want to render and
display polygons the Crimson Elan is much better and faster than the
HP720CRX.

The loaner DEC Alpha's we have had in here (one from DEC and the other
from Kubota Pacific) have been faster than both the Crimson and HP on
the various benchmarks I have run and are aware of, but I expect that
the HP735s will outperform the Alpha.

>	SGI is real sharp about keeping up with the latest and best
>compiler/optimizer tricks. They've also just generally done a pretty
>good job with optimizing various oft-called library routines(*).

Yes, SGI does a good job in the details.  Another area that they have
paid fair amount of attention to is I/O.  In general they did a very
good job in designing the I/O system in the new Onyx/Challenge/Power
Challenge product lines.  I already know of one application that will
run into an I/O bottleneck problem on the Onyx (but it is probably a
moot issue as I will not have the money to purchase an Onyx suitably
configured and options which would hit this bottleneck [anyone got
1 to 1.5 Million dollars they would care to share with me ;-) ]).

>	The compiler guys DEC has working on Alpha compilers are
>some really hot hackers. Remember that the Alpha compilers are a lot
>newer than the MIPS compiler suite - once they get ironed out, look
>for some major performance improvements.

I know that they are working on this, but I have no idea of when it will
be available.  

>	Moral:
>		Work smarter not harder.

We all need to do more of this, unfortunately it is not always obvious
where the smarter path lies.

>		Track compiler technologies.

You also need to be aware of implementation details.  One gotcha in the
Alpha architecture that I just found out, is that it is very poor at doing
context switches (there was a letter to editor in a recent issue of ACM
Communications that talked about this).  So, if you are looking for a
box for 20 simultaneous users, I would not recommend an Alpha based product
(well at least a single CPU version of it).  On the other hand, if it is
going to be used to run a single heavy duty code it may be the machine of
choice.

>		Profile and optimize libc and stdio.

For end users, you need to have some concept of what influences various
architectural features will have on your application.  It is always best
if you can run your application on the various choices before purchase.
Also just because X box is fastest (even on your application) does not
mean it is the best.

Good Luck & B Cing U

Buck

