Many thanks to all those who responded via e-mail.  The volume was big 
enough that I didn't have time to respond individually.

So, here is the summary of responses I received.  I knew before they 
started arriving that this was quite the can of worms to open--everybody
would be saying "Machine X is faster than Machine Y for the applications
that *I* am interested in"

Here are my insights:
- There were a number of responses saying HP is faster, some saying Alpha
  is faster, and a small number saying SGI's were very close.  This is
  a good illustration of SPECmarks and other industry standard benchmarks
  being a good starting point for machine performance, but your best bet
  is to run your own code on each machine and see which is faster.

- It was pointed out that the Alpha and HP top-end machines are quite
  expensive and SGI's may offer a better price/performance ratio.  For
  my situation we need as much horsepower as possible and price/performance
  is not as important as straight performance.

- One especially good quote from "Douglas A. Brune" <brune@luther.che.wisc.edu>:

     The bottom line is, decide which code you want to run fast, and
     arrange to benchmark it yourself on the machines. If you want
     a representative mix of real world code to run fast, believe
     the SPEC ratings.

- My original plan was to benchmark HP/DEC/SGI/IBM, but schedules have 
  been adjusted beyond my control and I am forced to make an educated
  guess :-)  Based on the above quote, I will be choosing between
  the DEC Alpha 3000/500X and the HP9000/735.  (It appears that I am
  one of the few users out there who has used HP-UX and likes it :-)

Here are the responses I received:

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

From: Jack Churchill <CHURCHILL@decus.COM.AU>

We recently bought three Indigo-2 machines after we bench tested our own
FORTRAN programs which involved complex calculations used by geophysicists.
I don't have the results because I wasn't directly involved - they belong to
