In article <1993May23.091409.20161@ugle.unit.no>, venaas@nvg.unit.no (Stig Venaas) writes:
> In article <1993May22.055529.5973@spcvxb.spc.edu> terry@spcvxb.spc.edu (Terry Kennedy, Operations Mgr.) writes:
>>[...] If you plug the CPU into
>>a non-C-D slot or into a system where something else is on the C-D bus,
>>you will almost certainly destroy the CPU board (and quite possibly any-
>>thing else which is on the C-D bus).
> 
> The CPU board can of course co-exist with other boards on the CD bus.
> But I think the CPU board should always be in the first slot followed
> by the RAM boards.

  I'm not sure if you're agreeing or disagreeing with me here - let me give
an example. If you use the BA11-style box with all 9 slots wired C-D, and
you have a KA630, an MS630, and then an RLV11 (2-board set using C-D for
private communications) it will not work and will likely destroy the CPU
board, which doesn't have very robust drivers for the C-D bus.

> As far as I know the only practical difference between the CD bus and
> the other, is that you don't need Grant Continuity cards on the CD bus.

  That's because there are no continuity paths opened by not having a board
in a slot, requiring a grant card. By the way, in the PDP-11/73 and /83, the
memory has to come first, then the CPU board, for C-D access (PMI memory) to
work. So, the order of things on the C-D bus is implementation-specific.

> BTW, is it possible to use the KA630 without RAM boards? I know,
> 1 Mb RAM, no VMS, no Ultrix etc. But it could be possible to use it
> for something else.

  It will power up and give you a ">>>" prompt. I suspect it will be able to
boot the diagnostics (at least older versions) and VAXeln (which is what the
diagnostics use).

> Also, does anyone know how much RAM there is on a KA650? And is 32Mb
> the max amount of RAM that can be used with it?

  There's no RAM on the KA650 CPU module. However, it does not have the 2-
board memory limit that the KA630 does - it will support at least 4 memory
boards. That means 2 in a BA23, 3 in a BA123, and 4 in a BA2xx. I've seen
BA2xx systems with 4 16Mb boards (64Mb total). I don't know if the KA650
handles denser boards (32Mb or 64Mb) or what the addressability limit is
for that processor. I can look it up when I get into the office, though.

