In article <1993May22.140213.73@decus.org.nz>, duffy@decus.org.nz writes:

|>
|>Does anybody know WHAT defines the maximum memory capacity of a Vax4100
|>to be 128MB ?
|>
|>In other words, when they are available, why couldn't 32MB SIMMS be
|>installed in the Vax4100 instead of the current 16MB SIMMS ?
|>
|>Thanks,
|>Jim.
|>
|>

The hardware design defines the max memory capacity.  Adding more memory
would either require more memory banks, which would require more simms,
or else each bank would have to be bigger, which would require more address
lines to each bank, and they simply aren't there.  This is not a unique
issue to the VAX 4000-100, but effects every computer out there.  You can
plan ahead only so far.  The memory controller used in the VAX 4000-100
was designed back in 1990, when 4Mb DRAMs were still on the horizon.

>>>Joe


