> This may be a reference to the paper by Jonathan Kay & Joseph Pasquale,
> "Measurement, Analysis, and Improvement of UDP/IP Throughput for the
> DECstation 5000", Proc. Winter 1993 USENIX Conf., San Diego, pp 249-258.
> They make a brief reference to "a new FDDI controller", presumably the
> DEFTA, and show how (if you're willing to live without UDP checksums!)
> one might get about 50 Mbit/sec.  Note that the DEFTA is NOT
> supported by ULTRIX yet; they were using an experimental driver.

Thanks for the mention!!  However, I would like to clear up a detail,
specifically the

> (if you're willing to live without UDP checksums!)

part.  There are one or two places where I described the Checksum
Redundancy Avoidance algorithm poorly, so I'd like to set the record
straight.  What's going on is not just turning off checksums.
Completely turning off checksums is not such a good idea because if
you send your data over a WAN or across a bunch of links corruption is
more likely to sneak in, though a lot of Unix boxes (not DECs) are
shipped that way. 

We proposed checksum redundancy avoidance as an improved alternative.
Checksums are only avoided when the networking software is fairly sure
it's redundant with a link-layer hardware checksum - when it's going
across a single LAN which includes CRC/checksum protection  as a
feature.  Ethernet and FDDI do support hardware CRCs, so TCP and UDP
checksums are avoided when crossing a single Ethernet or FDDI network.
Since most packets are sent across a single LAN, that algorithm
permits checksum avoidance in most cases.


On the other side of the coin, in all fairness, I should point out
that our 50/sec Mbits result was send-side UDP only with the nifty
DMA-ing adapter that you can only buy for Alphas as yet.  The
end-to-end throughputs we got were 33 Mbits/sec for UDP (corresponding
to NFS performance) and 25 Mbits/sec for TCP.  All that was on a
DECstation 5000/200.

An actual implementation for Ultrix and the paper in question can be
gotten by anonymous FTP from 

ucsd.edu:pub/csl/fastnet

							Jon


