Yes, but not as  good as one would like:

DEC OSF/1 T1.3-3, cc -migrate (aka GEM C) -O4:

   DEC 3000-500 	8.6 seconds

>	  d_z=(float)(k&0xfec);			16 %
>	  mpix[((int)d2_y<<10)-(int)d2_x]=k-1;  46 %

These two lines together account for about 60 % of the overall benchmark
time ! Byte access is not the culprit, changing the definition of mpix & pix
to "int" changes the benchmark time just to 8.4 seconds. 

What is hurting the Alpha is the conversion between integer and floating
point values. Because there are no instructions to move values between
floating point and integer registers, each conversion (apart from the
convert instructions) translates to a load and a store as well. Allthough
the merging write buffer gets a lot of that traffic caught it still ties
up the machine (from a raw cycle perspective "pixie" would predict this
beast to go in 2.2 seconds, but it actually is *memory-bound* on the Alpha).

