In article <1993May16.160003.1@gsbacd.uchicago.edu>, gft_robert@gsbacd.uchicago.edu (opcode ranger) writes:
> This is probably in a FAQ somewhere, and I don't mean to add to newsgroup
> bandwidth, but I fairly quickly need to know how to do the following: I need to
> set up a mail forwarding address from a VAX VMS system to another computer
> across the network.  It's simply to set up to another cluster computer, just
> doing
> 
> MAIL> SET FORWARD NODE::USER
> 
> or some such.  
> 
> 
> I wish to forward mail from another computer to this account.  The address used
> from that site to reach me here is:
> 
> bb1t::uucp%"gft_robert@gsbacd.uchicago.edu

    Point 1: That address _should_ have had a training double-quote mark
	     although in certain instances, DCL allows it to be omitted.

    Point 2: In giving SET FORWARD an address, or MAIL for that matter, 
	     you've got to hide the at-sign by enclosing the whole address, 
	     or at least the Internet part, in matched double quotes.
	     That's why the address you've supplied has 'em.

    Point 3: Since you've got to get embedded double-quote marks into the
	     forwarding address, use the Fortran convention of doubling
	     up the embedded double-quote marks.

    Result:

	MAIL> SET FORWARD "BB1T::UUCP%""gft_robert@gsbacd.uchicago.edu"""


