	Let me back up a second and try to cut through the smoke. When
HP, DEC, and IBM stood up and announced OSF, they were announcing that
they were going to start an open process for technology procurement,
which all the vendors were going to share. They were going to choose
one operating system platform, and they were going to base all their
offerings on it.

	Now, vendors sharing a common technology has been done. But
it's never been done well. There's 2 ways to do it:

	1) License it all from a common source who controls the implementation.
		This is the way NFS licensing is done. Each vendor takes the
		NFS code from Sun and hacks and hammers it into their offering.
		By the time they've done it, what they've got is totally them
		specific and when Sun comes out with another release of the
		code they either can't use it because it would cost too much
		to update their pool, or they have to redo the work and re-add
		all their bugs. Another good example of this is X-windows.
		MIT releases a new version of X. 2 years later, vendors
		support it on most of their graphics boards. Yucch.

	2) All work constantly from the same pool. This means that all the
		vendors using the code have to feed back changes and fixes.
		Once the base product is developed then everyone works from
		it.

	So, OSF finally picks AIX (according to IBM) and then changes its
mind because everyone screamed - it looked too bogus for anyone to believe
that OSF had co-incidentally picked a member's operating systems technology,
and the whole fabrication teetered for a moment. I'm sure that if AIX was
OSF's kernel technology, HP and DEC would have bailed then and there and
we'd have been spared all the shouting. Remember ACE?

	OOps, OSF realizes that AIX won't do, so they pick Mach, which is
nice and vendor neutral, though it has the disadvantage of being broken
and vaporous. OSF ploughs loads of effort into making it work somewhat.
This becomes OSF's SDK and has support for MIPS, Encore multimax and
the 386. DEC gets a cut of the tape and ploughs loads of work into it.
OSF keeps working, fixing some stuff and breaking other stuff. DEC
decides that the version of NFS sucks and re-inserts Sun's version. DEC
decides the VM system sucks and largely re-writes it. OSF meanwhile
plugs cheerfully along, fixes some of the VM bugs (or claims to) and
improves NFS (or claims to) so now they're two totally different
puppies. OSF starts working on microkernels. By now DEC's stuff is so
completely different they'd have to start all over if they wanted to
use OSF's stuff - they begin waffling about "OSF's microkernel is a
research project still."   IBM and HP continue frying their own fish
elsewhere, and watch from a distance doing nothing other than paying
lipservice to the whole ongoing fiasco. IBM is ploughing work into
AIX, improving it and working on advanced stuff like JFS and clustering.

	So, now we get the "OSF's not about kernel technology - really"
shift. Well, that's partly because OSF bagged out of the operating
systems business because they really were just being a bunch of
dilettantes and it was becoming increasingly obvious to everyone that
nothing that came out of OSF was remotely commercial quality. Poor
DEC is left holding the bag, being the only vendor who is actually
shipping anything based on something descended from OSF. (though
the DEC OSF code has nothing to do with OSF's OSF code anymore other
than that there's yet another line in the copyright headers, and most
likely another license fee tacked on)  So HP and IBM both nod and state
that they're committed to eventually comply with the OSF API. Goody.
POSIX. That was hard. And, yes, if someone ports DCE, DFC, DMR and
DDT to their platforms they'll sell them.

