In article <1seem2INNbha@rex.graphics.cornell.edu> mkc@Graphics.Cornell.edu (Mitch Collinsworth) writes:
>>A recent DEC Press release announced Turbochannel FDDI controllers
>>with improved performance so before buying 700-c for Alpha I asked
>>about the performance improvement. Our local sales office passed me
>>onto a Networking Expert who said the new board could sustain 110 Mbps
>>whereas the 700 was limited to about 50 Mbps (both on Alpha).
>
>Wow!  110% efficiency.  Now THAT'S something to brag about.

Yes, we like to go that extra mile for our customers :-).

Actually, I believe that the 110 Mbps figure comes from some preliminary
measurements using *two* DEFTAs on one system (i.e., two separate FDDI
rings).  But don't quote me on that!

>We recently had a DEC network expert here (who *really* was an expert).
>Amoung lots of other useful info he passed on, he said the best DEC has
>seen the 700-type interface do is 27 Mb/s.  Supposedly someone else
>tested it and reportedly got 37 Mb/s, but DEC doesn't know how they got
>it to do that.

This may be a reference to the paper by Jonathan Kay & Joseph Pasquale,
"Measurement, Analysis, and Improvement of UDP/IP Throughput for the
DECstation 5000", Proc. Winter 1993 USENIX Conf., San Diego, pp 249-258.
They make a brief reference to "a new FDDI controller", presumably the
DEFTA, and show how (if you're willing to live without UDP checksums!)
one might get about 50 Mbit/sec.  Note that the DEFTA is NOT supported
by ULTRIX yet; they were using an experimental driver.

It's not quite fair to say that "DEC doesn't know how they got it to
do that".  The DEC people that you have talked with didn't know; DEC
is (still) a large company and not everyone knows everything.

>As for the NEW interface, at some interoperability convention, DEC was
>approached by another computer company with the taunt, "Hey, our FDDI
>adapter is faster than *your* FDDI adapter."  DEC said essentially,
>"That's nice, we don't really know how fast ours is.  How can you test
>them?"  The other company proposed the test, they tested out somewhere
>around 50 Mb/s or so, and DEC tested out at 94 Mb/s.

Interesting story.  The truth is that this challenge was made sometime
last year, several months before the Fall '92 Interop.  A number of
DEC engineers decided to meet the challenge, and at that Interop were
showing a pair of DECstation-3000/500 systems, connected via DEFTAs,
doing TCP at just slightly below the theoretical maximum (a smidge
higher than 94 Mbit/sec, as I recall).  This was certainly not a
surprise, it was the result of a lot of hard work and careful measurement
at the intermediate stages.

It's also true that "the other company" involved had, by that time,
also managed to get their implementation up to full FDDI speed.  So
it was a good thing that the DEC team decided to keep going once they
passed the 50 Mbit/sec level!

For much more information about how this performance was achieved,
see "High Performance TCP/IP for OSF/1 Alpha AXP Workstations",
available via anonymous FTP from gatekeeper.dec.com
	/pub/DEC/DECinfo/whitepaper/hiperf-tcp.{abs,ps}

>Sounds about right.  I saw a tech article somewhere (yes, actually
>published by DEC) that if memory serves, claimed about a 2x speed gain
>for NFS via FDDI .vs. ethernet, using DS5000 and "old" FDDI adapter.
>(I'm trying to remember where I saw this, but so far I haven't a clue.)

So it says in a table in the 1992-1993 "Network Buyer's Guide".  But
I wouldn't put much faith in this particular table, since it seems to
be based on a bunch of undocumented assumptions.  In any case, it's
quite hard to know how NFS performance will vary with network bandwidth
in any given situation.

Disclaimer: I was not a direct participant in any of the engineering
work reported here, so I could have some of the details wrong.

-Jeff


