In article 7117@npri6.npri.com, murphy@npri6.npri.com (David P. Murphy) writes:
>TRUNTZ@UHAVAX.HARTFORD.EDU (TRUNTZ, ERIC) writes:
>
>>Everyone,
>>   and the location of the user is reasonable simple. My problem arises
>>   in determining which imagename to select for a user. Suppose a user
>>   has logged in and his process name is USER then he spawns a subprocess
>>   called USER_1 he now has two processes on the system. At this stage
>>   all keystrokes that the user makes will end up affecting the process
>>   named USER_1. This is what I mean when I say a termianl is communicating
>>   with a process. Suppose the usaer types ATTACH USER, Now keystrokes
>>   get "sent" to the process call USER. My question is how can I determine
>>   which process a terminal is communicating with.
>
>assuming that you really have made it this far, the first test you can make
>is choose the process which does *not* have a JPI$_STATE equal to SCH$C_HIB
>or SCH$C_HIBO.
>
>in other words, the process which is not attached will be hibernating.
>of course, the active process may very well be hibernating on its own accord,
>but that's the best i can do for now.

Not necessarily.  Have you ever used the SPAWN/NOWAIT command?  This creates a subprocess that shares the terminal with the parent process.  I believe input (and output) alternates between the parent process and the subprocess based on the order read requests are queued to the I/O channel associated with the terminal.  In this case, neither process enters the hibernate state.

