I've got a seed Alpha 3000/500 running OSF 1.2.  Part of our evaluation
is based on having the ability to keep track of CPU time and disk use
of the users - since the Powers That Be have decreed that there ain't no
more free computing here....

I've suffered through the barely usable accounting in Ultrix, and beaten
my head bloody on the useless accounting in AIX (well, as of 3.2 it finally
works), and been fairly happy with accounting in Irix (but I wish SGI would
update it so the disk accounting worked with NIS), so I'd hoped that a new
"thoroughly modern" OS like OSF/1 might actually have a fully-functional
accounting package finally.


Well, I've gone through the man pages and set up accounting as described, i.e.
edited /usr/spool/cron/crontabs/adm and added the lines:

    0 2 * * *               /usr/sbin/acct/runacct > /usr/adm/acct/nite/fd2log &
    5 * * * *               /usr/sbin/acct/ckpacct &
    0 3 1 * *               /usr/sbin/acct/monacct &

(ignoring disk accounting for now.)

Unfortunately, this doesn't generate any output files.  Being (so I thought)
clever, I ran some of these commands by hand.  Once, runacct coredumped with
a segmentation fault; a few times it gave unaligned access errors in acctcon2 
and acctprc1; occasionally it appears to run without errors - but _only_ when 
run manually, not from cron.

What is particularly annoying - aside from it not working, I mean - is that
when I took a look at the output generated when I ran it manually, it doesn't
report the CPU time used!  Instead - just like AIX 3.1 - the best I can get is
the _memory_ use per user (which I don't care about), and the CPU use  _per 
command_ (which I also can't get any usefulinformation from).


I hate to flame people, but this was dumb when IBM did it this way, and I
thought DEC was smarter.  Or is it just that I'm missing something here
(in which case I apologize for the mis-directed flame, and humbly request
enlightenment)?

When will accounting work in a useful way for OSF/1?

