Tone != flame, but Tone == should talk facts....

Digital sells an add-on package of converters (as noted by a previous
poster to this very thread) called the CDA Converter Library.  This
library takes perhaps the most rational approach I've seen in
converter technology (and I note that many players in the game have
started to architect their solutions similarly): CDA becomes the
central format with source/destination converters for each other
format desired.  This way, you write two converter pieces (front and
back ends) and you can converter between any other formats for which
you have converters.

Back when I was working on VAX/VMS systems (about 2 years ago), the
library had converters for most common formats and a few "strange"
ones.  I assume this number has grown (for example, the previous
poster mentioned RTF format, so Word should be a snap).

The reformatting required at that point is small or even non-existant.

DECwrite is one of the best writing tools I've seen, since it provides
support for style sheets et al - any good company has printing
standards and style sheets help maintain them in the face of the rest
of us:-).  DECwrite also supports live-links to applications and data
(back when that first started, it was big news - now Microsoft has
finally over the last year put in place OLE (DDE is also becoming
common)).

The CDA Converter Library allows you to take DECwrite documents almost
anyplace, so ....  Too bad you didn't know about that add-on for VMS
back when you had to convert all those documents, eh (We used the
library for just such a thing [to Macs even] at a previous employer
and it worked well)?

BTW, I'm not associated in any way with Digital (I get to touch a
DECstation and VAXstation occasionally for debugging X problems), but
I like VMS a bit and think Digital sometimes writes good software
(I'll decide what I think of current hardware building when I touch an
Alpha system:-).

Have fun! - Bob

