In article <1993May4.173623.3560@gps.leeds.ac.uk>, daniel@scs.leeds.ac.uk (D N Crow) writes:
> I have recently moved Universities, and as a result I need to convert my
> (nearly completed) thesis from DECWrite v1.1 (British) format into a format
> that Microsoft Word (v5.0 on the Mac) can read.
> 
> DECwrite seems only capable of exporting documents as DECWrite, EPS or CDA
> (DEC's Compound Document Architecture) formats: is there any way I can get the
> text (with as much formatting as possible, and maybe even with diagrams, though
> these are less vital) of my thesis to something that Word reads (like RTF)?

We recently installed the CDA Converter Library 2.0 product for trial, and
it does exactly this sort of thing.  You simply need to save the file in CDA
format (DDIF?) and say:

$ CONVERT/DOCUMENT INFILE.DDIF/FORMAT=DDIF OUTFILE.RTF/FORMAT=RTF

You can choose RTF or WORD5 format, but we have had more success with RTF.

The Word and RTF converters are new for V2.0 of the library, so they aren't
in V1.1.

I have also heard rumours that a new version of Word (the next one?) will
be able to read and write CDA format.

Good luck!
