I've found a strange little bug with DEC Ultrix 4.2A's rlogin that I'd like 
to share with you guys...  Any ideas on why this is happening?  Is this a
known bug?

If a user has a large .rhosts file (I'm not sure quite how long, I haven't
experimented with this aspect too much; long here is ~120 lines...), that 
user can log into the system with nothing being recorded in /etc/utmp, nor 
in /usr/adm/wtmp by simply rlogging into the system.  If the machine that 
the user is rlogging in from is not in .rhosts and the user fails to enter
his or her password right on the first try, the session will be put into 
wtmp and utmp...  

Miscellaneous ramifications of this:

   - Since there's no entry for that person in utmp, who/w/etc won't show
     the user as being logged in, although (of course) processes are still
     shown when a ps is done...
   - Likewise, programs like biff/comsat and others will fail to see the 
     person as logged on...
   - The person will not be able to access any files of any groups he or she
     is in...
   - Any programs that do user authentication with getlogin() or similar 
     procedures that look in /etc/utmp will fail..
   - Programs that access wtmp for data (last, general accounting progs...) 
     will not be correct...  Other accounting programs that use real
     accounting files are unaffected...

Just a little something strange I discovered.  :)

   - Ed

