In update 10, I have detected exactly two problems with the compiler, and
I've run a lot of stuff through it.

1.  On some of our code, it warns us that to optimize it, we need to do
    -Olimit with a larger number to increase the number of basic blocks,
    but goes on and generates unoptimized code.  If you try to up the
    limit, some modules cause the compiler to dump core (making a corefile
    in excess of 200 megabytes).

2.  Having a switch with no curly brace compound statement never notices
    the labels, e.g.....

	switch(i)
case 4:		if(i < 100)
case 2:			if(i < 50)
case 1:				if( i < 25)
					foo(0);
				else foo(1)
			else foo(2)
		else foo(3)

     It never executes any of the code in the if statements.  This is the
     only compiler I've ever seen this fail on (it works on the MIPS compiler).
     Wrapping { } around all the lines after the switch(i) causes it to work
     properly.  I know this is an usual (but it is legal both in old K&R and
     in ANSI) C construct.

By and large, pretty minor problems.  The latest release (10) code for the
ALPHA is as good (if not better) than most of the production releases from
other vendors.  It's really complete and up to date (which is more than I
can say about some vendors).  It even has EMACS already compiled for you
and other fun things like RCS...

And they are fast.
-Ron


