Respondent says he enters code into memory from the console and then directs
the CPU to start executing there, but nothing happens.

I can imagine a number of possible problems (though I have never used that
computer and cannot verify any of them).

- one must (in general) flush the data cache to memory and clear the
  instruction cache to force the new instructions to be read from memory; this
  is a general characteristic of most machines with separate I and D caches
- the region must be appropriately mapped: there must be an appropriate entry
  loaded in the instruction translation lookaside buffer (ITLB) and/or the
  ITLB miss handler must be willing to load one up for you if/when you fault
  on it
- the system may avoid mapping certain parts of low memory as a technique for
  faulting on most attempts to fetch through nil pointers

