A recent DEC Press release announced Turbochannel FDDI controllers
with improved performance so before buying 700-c for Alpha I asked
about the performance improvement. Our local sales office passed me
onto a Networking Expert who said the new board could sustain 110 Mbps
whereas the 700 was limited to about 50 Mbps (both on Alpha). The new
board will be available for fibre optic soon , for copper when a standard
has emerged for UTP or STP cabling (End of 93?) - Thin wire likely to
be dropped.  Copper boards cannot work full duplex so expect max
throughput of about 90 Mbps.


>	- system load: Is the cpu a bottleneck (5k/240)
>	- standards: Does it work with other than DEC's concentrators?
>
>All available info appreciated
>

We are currently running 5 x 5k/240 and 1 x 5k/200 on 700-C, 
one of the 5k/240 is set up as a gateway to our Ethernet.

I found an nfsstone program on a ftp site but been unable to get any
meaningful measurements from it.

I have tried the following:-

server and client 5k/240 64Mbyte 700c copper FDDI
file_a is 1901056 bytes
file_b is 3802112 bytes

1) on our file server 
cp file_a /dev/null  ( to get file into cache )
0.001u 0.324s 0:01.53 26.9% 12+12k 234+1io 0pf 0w
cp file_a /dev/null ( check that it is in cache)
0.003u 0.167s 0:00.07 228.5% 13+13k 0+2io 0pf 0w

2) on NFS client
cp ~/.login /dev/null (to get cp into local cache)
cp file_a /dev/null (copying file_a from server cache to local /dev/null)
0.003u 0.265s 0:01.07 24.2% 13+13k 234+1io 0pf 0w

3) on our file server 
cp file_b /dev/null  ( to get file into cache )
cp file_b /dev/null ( check that it is cache)
0.003u 0.324s 0:00.23 139.1% 12+12k 0+0io 0pf 0w

4) on NFS client
cp ~/.login /dev/null (to get cp into local cache)
cp file_b /dev/null (copying file_b from server cache to local /dev/null)
0.042u 0.527s 0:02.11 26.5% 12+12k 466+1io 0pf 0w
cp file_b /dev/null (now copying from local cache to /dev/null)
0.011u 0.308 0:00.31 100% 13+13k 0+0io 0pf 0w

5) on Ethernet connected client 5k/240 64Mbyte
cp ~/.login /dev/null (to get cp into local cache)
cp file_b /dev/null (copying file_b from server cache to local /dev/null)
0.015u 0.433s 0:04.98 8.8% 12+12k 466+1io 0pf 0w

This seems to indicate a maximum NFS performance via FDDI ( from
remote cache to /dev/null) of the order of 2 Mbytes/sec as against
Ethernet 0.76 Mbytes/sec.

I am surprised by the low figure for FDDI - or is my measurement
meaningless.

Comments on this are very welcome.

